based on those  labels and my model didn't perform well  and  instead of basing um my model on  subjective labels i decided to enrich my  data set um by using objective labels  where  characters were overlaid on  a plain background and for  this type of data i had a clear standard  standard that defined objectively which  characters are readable and which are  not  and so i did a series of experiments  where i tried to balance  my data um during the training and in  the end i got a model i was satisfied  from  so how does  the whole system work  so the system gets  an input image and a text  and after that  the model craft runs  and  it extracts  basically each character into um  an image a small image  on each one of those characters a binary  classifier runs and classifies each  character into a readable or not  readable character  and  after that we calculate the  the contrast ratio bad contrast ratio  out of the entire characters in the text  and in the end we use a threshold in  order to classify the text  into a readable or not readable text  so  until this point i told you about how i  started  this project of  classifying a text overlaid on the  complex background into a text that is  accessible or not  and in the beginning i tried to  use different ocr architectures to solve  my problem and i told you how i got into  problems using this approach  and so i decided instead of  going  and complicating my solution i decided  to reframe my problem and this led to a  breakthrough and to a solution  and we can see now how it looks in one  of the products  of our company so basically this is  a product called site scanner  it  scans complete domains of our clients  for accessibility issues  and one of the issues that it detects is  the issue that  that i'm talking with you about and so  here we can see some of the results of  text that are really  and not clear and not readable and not  accessible  and  when i run um my system on like big  amounts of data i discove